WEBVTT

-1:59:59.500 --> 00:00:00.000
Yeah.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Colin, this was not no particular brand I would call out on stocking.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.900
I think this was across the board.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.560
It was not just in one thing.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.460
It was not just a single geography, but multiple geographies.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:20.660
And I would not attribute this to a specific drug, the stocking.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:21.160
Yeah.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.860
And maybe just to provide a little bit of color as well.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:30.060
I mean, this was related to the implementation of our new SAP system,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:33.260
where it's often the case when we roll that out in multiple geographies,

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.660
we do have to shift stocking levels for the cutover to the new SAP system.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.560
So that's the driver.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:41.360
And the reason why,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.660
it's not associated with one brand per se.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:46.040
Thanks.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:47.740
Thank you.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.240
Your next question comes from the line of Michael Leuchten from Jefferies.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.140
Please go ahead.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:56.640
Thank you for the follow-up.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:04.440
Vas, interested in your Semblix comment about the second half aiming for NBRX leadership in the US.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.440
Is that just natural progression of the dynamics that we're seeing

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.740
or is there a pivot point that would inflect that further?

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:13.240
Yeah.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:13.940
Thanks, Michael.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.440
I think, I think, yes, it's just the momentum we're seeing.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.440
I also think that,

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.540
you know, now we've gotten very strong access position for the brand.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.540
And so I think that stronger access position as it flows through.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.140
I mean, one of the things with CML is because it is a rare disease

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.440
and there's a limited number of newly diagnosed patients in a given year.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.040
It just takes time.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.840
And so any of the smaller fluctuations that you see quarter on quarter,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.640
is driven by very few patients.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:54.120
But all indications we're seeing is that given the very strong safety profile that physicians are seeing with the drug and the known efficacy profile, there's just a lot of momentum now.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.020
So that gives us confidence that we'll get to that market leadership position in the U.S.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:08.180
And I'll flag again, I mentioned in my opening comments that we're really just at the beginning now of moving from third line to first line ex-U.S.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:17.120
And I think one of the things that's been a positive trend as well, there are multiple generic medicines available in that first line setting.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:32.700
There seems to be a strong demand from physicians, but also payers are accepting the fact that Semblitz has demonstrated that it is a superior medicine in that front line setting and more openness to give us the reimbursement we would expect for such a medicine.

